Z||000000||rockcrawl||07-18-2000||10:12 PM||||Hi,<BR> I recently finnished swapping a 3800 V6 into my '86 GT. Let me just say it is awesome!! I have put about 1000 miles on the car. But I do have one problem. The exhaust sometimes stinks. It just started doing it within the last 200 miles or so. I can't describe the smell except that it's awfull, and I believe it's the catalytic converter. The entire exhaust system is new, everything is wired correctly, and all the emisions equipment is connected. No fault codes have been tripped in the ECM, and the engine never skips a beat. What makes it smell so bad? <P>By the way, the car is for sale for $4500. For details, check my site <A HREF="http://clik.to/rockcrawl" TARGET=_blank>http://clik.to/rockcrawl</A>  <P>Thanks,<BR>Jon<P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 07-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick||07-19-2000||12:18 AM||||<BR>A rotten egg smell is a sign of the cat failing. A cat can be poisoned by too rich a mixture, excessive oil burning, antifreeze from an internal coolant leak, etc. <P>The cat on my Fiero smells like this when the engine's cold, but once it's warmed up it appears to be fine. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||80's BOY||07-19-2000||12:31 AM||||just hope it's not your wiring harness rubbing your crossover exhaust pipe!! i got learned the hard way back in the day.||||reg||4
